    "profile_text": {
        "body": "Arm is a public company founded in 1990 and headquartered in Cambridge, United Kingdom. According to its own site, it provides compute platforms for AI from cloud to edge, and describes its platform as connecting cloud, edge, and physical AI with consistent software, flexible compute, and an ecosystem built to scale intelligence everywhere. The site says Arm is trusted by innovators worldwide and that it delivers performance, efficiency, and flexibility for uses including cloud infrastructure, personal devices, AI-defined vehicles, and robotics.\n\nAmong the specific offerings named on the site are the Arm AI Portal, which it describes as giving developers and agents one place to discover optimized models, evaluate performance, access deployment workflows, or optimize their own models for Arm, and the AGI CPU, discussed by Arm's Eddie Ramirez in connection with agentic AI and data center efficiency. The site also references NVIDIA's Open Agent Safety Platform as an example of Arm powering agentic AI, NVIDIA DGX Spark built on Arm-based NVIDIA Grace Blackwell technology for running large AI models locally, and Arm Create events in Shanghai and Shenzhen.\n\nThe held record lists no products, lawsuits, or SEC or patent facts for Arm. It does list several MCP servers under names that include Arm-related strings, such as ai.anomalyarmor/armor-mcp, ai.calendarmcp/server, ai.contractorsnearme/contractors-near-me, ai.harmny/harmny, ai.karmafor/karma-library, ai.smithery/hjsh200219-pharminfo-mcp, app.nanocorp.bidswarm/bidswarm, bid.scope/pharma, and cloud.aevia/warmnight-fishing, with descriptions covering data observability, calendar access, contractor search, career frameworks, a Karma library, Korean drug data, an ad board, pharma vendor services, and a night-fishing game.",
